          "B": "Research an independent full-distance selection algorithm for larger neighborhoods. Compute distance records and use robust partial selection rather than sorting all N points. Preserve cutoff ties through a (distance, encounter ordinal) key, or select a distance threshold and resolve its equal-distance boundary in original encounter order. Never depend on partition stability. Use robust pivoting and a fallback to prevent quadratic degeneration; account for the additional O(N) workspace and allocation-size overflow. Establish a serial candidate first. If supported by the unchanged build and remaining budget, investigate ARM64 vectorization across independent points and up to four workers for distance generation, preserving reference floating-point evaluation. Measure whether parallel overhead or memory traffic erases the gain.",
          "C": "Research input and storage throughput independently of selection: initially leave knn unchanged. Replace repeated scanf calls with buffered fread-based parsing and verified float conversion, such as strtof, rather than an approximate fixed-decimal parser. Preserve the source grammar, including n_groups=, raw label characters, valid whitespace, signs, exponents and float rounding. Handle tokens crossing buffer boundaries, EOF and non-seekable stdin. Because k and the query arrive after all groups, retain the coordinates; do not assume distances can be computed while reading. Investigate contiguous storage and fewer allocations while preserving group traversal order and compatible interfaces where practical. Report end-to-end gains with the original selector so parsing benefits are distinguishable from algorithmic changes.",
          "G": "Integrate only correctness-preserving candidates produced by A/B/C. Keep the baseline and each independent winner available. Within the existing evaluation budget, compare the winning selectors both with and without C's parser/storage changes; measure threading separately where available. Do not assume improvements combine additively. Use only justified runtime-size dispatch between algorithms, never recognized test values, labels or expected outputs. Audit distance rounding, encounter-order cutoff ties, label ordering, padding, allocation arithmetic and NaN fallbacks across interfaces. Select using the unchanged harness's end-to-end measurements and prescribed repetitions on the fixed four-core ARM64 target. Revert regressions and report measured lineage and correctness status. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler/build configuration, budgets, target or permissions.",
          "analysis": "The target is the supplied KNN program; A/B/C are research roles, not the other official problems. With N total points, the baseline performs O(Nk) neighbor maintenance, O(k log k) label sorting and repeated scanf parsing. The only timing evidence is a valid baseline averaging 2.0080 seconds over three samples (2.0066–2.0094), with four correctness cases; there is no size distribution or bottleneck profile. Therefore investigate selection and parsing independently. All candidates must preserve float squared-distance evaluation, earlier encounter order at equal-distance cutoffs, smallest-label vote ties under compare_for_sort's target plain-char ordering, and the single-character output. Match floating-point contraction/rounding rather than introducing approximations or reassociation. NaN distances do not obey the ordering assumed by heap/partition selection: retain a reference-equivalent fallback. Preserve sentinel-label padding for supported k>N inputs. Four passing cases alone do not establish these invariants. This is one query, supplied after the points, so spatial-index construction is not a priority."
